{"t":1788738858,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"f09af0bd-79ba-4a39-8f81-337b832ebce1","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-06T18:46:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 061846\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n246 PM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo significant changes to the forecast from what was issued this\nmorning, just added details in Key Message 2 regarding potential\nstorms on Thursday. Uncertainty remains high at this point, but\ngiven the right timing of the frontal passage, a severe threat\ncould unfold. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Drier conditions with seasonable temperatures and lower\n  humidity today into early parts of the week.\n\n- 2) Shower and thunderstorm chances return by mid week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Drier conditions with seasonable temperatures \nand lower humidity Sunday into early next week.\n\nBroad high pressure over the Great Lakes will track over the \nMid-Atlantic through Monday. This will bring a change in \nairmass with notably lower dew points. Highs in the low 80s for \nmost today and Monday. Overnight lows in the 50s and low 60s.\n\nSurface high pressure begins to transition south of the Mid- \nAtlantic on Tuesday as upper level ridging builds over the\nsoutheast CONUS. Temperatures likely begin to creep up again \nduring this time, accompanied by increasing humidity as mid-\nlevel dry air tracks south of the region with more humid air \ncoming in from the north.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower and thunderstorm chances return by mid week.\n\nUpper-level ridging over the Appalachians starts to flatten into \nzonal flow beginning Wednesday. As this occurs, a trough forming \nover Quebec will begin to track east across the northeast CONUS, \nwith a stemming cold front moving across the East Coast by Thursday. \nTemperatures gradually increase during this time, along with \nincreasing humidity ahead of the front. By Thursday, temps \npotentially reach the upper 80s to low 90s. \n\nAs this frontal boundary tracks through the Mid-Atlantic, showers\n& thunderstorms are possible across the region, beginning Wednesday\nevening and into Thursday. The 12z guidance remains unclear in \nterms of timing and the exact evolution of this system, so a lot\nof uncertainties remain. There is general agreement in trough\ndigging south into the region, but the exact strength and \ntiming of that trough is still being ironed out. Temperatures \nThursday are expected to be in the 80s to near 90, with dew \npoint in the 60s to perhaps low 70s. This should yield plenty of\nCAPE south of the front itself and allow for at least some \nthunderstorm development if the forcing arrives near peak \nheating. The severe threat is still in question, but remains \nnon-zero. If the trough tracks far enough south, there could be \na fair amount of wind shear to work with as well, which would \nincrease that severe threat. Some of the AI guidance \n(particularly the WxNext) do highlight our region, so it is \ncertainly something to keep a close eye on. Will continue to \nmonitor latest guidance in hopes of honing in on a more \nconfident solution. \n\nAs this cold front transitions offshore towards the end of next \nweek, temps drop again & conditions become much drier heading into \nthe weekend as high pressure moves in overhead. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /18Z SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nLight showers and drizzle linger near CHO this morning, and will\nlikely continue to do so over the next couple hours. This should\ntaper off into the early afternoon. \n\nThereafter, VFR conditions should persist through Wednesday \nevening.\n\nShowers & thunderstorms associated with an incoming cold front \ncould temporarily bring sub-VFR conditions to terminals by\nThursday afternoon. Ahead of the front on Thursday, winds could\ngust between 20-25 knots before diminishing overnight. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nNo marine hazards are expected through the holiday weekend. \nGenerally light flow is expected, though onshore gusts could \napproach marginal SCA levels late this afternoon. Currently\nfavoring a Marine Weather Statement given brief duration.\n\nNortheasterly winds Monday shift southerly on Tuesday, \nremaining below SCA levels.\n\nSCAs are possible across the Bay by Wednesday evening ahead of \nan incoming cold front. Winds could remain elevated throughout \nThursday as this frontal boundary tracks east across the coast.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTidal anomalies remain elevated over the next several days due\nto an onshore flow. Minor tidal flooding is becoming\nincreasingly likely at Annapolis, and could also be possible in\nthe other sensitive tidal locations in the northern Chesapeake \nBay. Will continue to monitor as the current high tide ends and\ntransitions to low tide. If the forecast holds, may need to\nissue an advisory later this evening. \n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...CJL/CPB\nAVIATION...CJL/CPB\nMARINE...CJL/CPB\n"},"forecast":{"t":1788738828,"days":{"2026-09-06":{"hi":null,"lo":64,"pop":5,"short":"Partly Cloudy"},"2026-09-07":{"hi":84,"lo":63,"pop":4,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-08":{"hi":86,"lo":66,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-09":{"hi":89,"lo":73,"pop":3,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-10":{"hi":91,"lo":69,"pop":54,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-11":{"hi":82,"lo":65,"pop":17,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Slight Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-12":{"hi":86,"lo":68,"pop":23,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Slight Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-13":{"hi":88,"lo":null,"pop":10,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1788735600,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,9900,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,n